            {
              "_key": "k134",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k135",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Under full ratchet, the Series A fund is fully protected: its ₹15 crore is still worth ₹15 crore at the new price. Every one of its 3,00,000 extra shares comes out of the founders, the ESOP pool, the seed investors and even the new Series B investor. Under broad-based weighted average, the fund recovers only a small part of its markdown, and the pain is shared far more evenly.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_type": "callout",
              "type": "warning",
              "text": "In this simplified example, the extra anti-dilution shares also dilute the Series B investor. In practice, a new investor usually insists that those shares are counted in its pre-money share count, which pushes all of the cost back onto founders and earlier unprotected holders.",
              "_key": "k136"
            },
            {
              "_type": "comparison",
              "title": "Full ratchet vs broad-based weighted average",
              "columns": [
                {
                  "_key": "k138",
                  "label": "Full ratchet",
                  "points": [
                    "Conversion price falls to the new round price",
                    "Ignores how much money the down round raises",
                    "Protected investor fully restored at the new price",
                    "Can devastate founders and unprotected holders",
                    "Rare in balanced Indian term sheets; a sign of weak negotiating power"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k140",
                  "label": "Broad-based weighted average",
                  "points": [
                    "Conversion price falls in proportion to the size of the down round",
                    "A small down round causes a small adjustment",
                    "Protected investor recovers only part of the markdown",
                    "Dilution shared more fairly",
                    "The most common market standard"
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "_key": "k141"
            },
            {
              "_type": "exercise",
              "variant": "numericInput",
              "title": "Calculate the adjusted conversion price",
              "prompt": "An investor holds CCPS with a conversion price of ₹300 and broad-based weighted average protection. The company has 10,00,000 fully diluted shares and raises ₹5 crore in a down round at ₹200 per share. What is the adjusted conversion price, in ₹?",
              "correctValue": 280,
              "tolerance": 0.5,
              "unit": "₹",
              "explanation": "A = 10,00,000. B = ₹5 crore ÷ ₹300 = 1,66,667. C = ₹5 crore ÷ ₹200 = 2,50,000. CP₂ = ₹300 × (10,00,000 + 1,66,667) ÷ (10,00,000 + 2,50,000) = ₹300 × 0.9333 = ₹280. Under full ratchet it would have been ₹200.",
              "_key": "k142"
            },
            {
              "_type": "exercise",
              "variant": "quiz",
              "title": "Most founder-friendly",
              "question": "Which form of anti-dilution protection is usually the most favourable to founders and unprotected shareholders?",
              "options": [
                "Full ratchet",
                "Narrow-based weighted average",
                "Broad-based weighted average",
                "All three have the same effect"
