              "title": "The angel holding plain equity",
              "scenario": "You invested ₹10 lakh in PedalPower's seed round, but unlike the seed fund, you took equity shares, not CCPS. You now learn that the Series B down round is proceeding at ₹250 per share, and that the Series A fund has full ratchet protection. The founders ask all existing shareholders to approve the round at a general meeting.",
              "prompt": "How does this affect you, and what can you realistically do?",
              "modelAnswer": "How it affects you:\n1. You are diluted twice: once by the 4,00,000 new Series B shares and again by the 3,00,000 extra shares the Series A fund receives under full ratchet.\n2. As an equity holder, you also sit behind every CCPS series in any sale, and the Series A fund's preference is unaffected by the down round.\n3. Your ₹200 entry price is below ₹250, so your shares are still above cost on paper, but your percentage falls much more than it would under weighted average protection.\n\nWhat you can realistically do:\n1. Ask the founders and Series A fund whether they will negotiate the ratchet down to broad-based weighted average, or waive it, to make the rescue round fair. Series A investors sometimes agree when the alternative is the company running out of cash.\n2. Ask whether the extra anti-dilution shares will be counted in the Series B pre-money, and who bears them.\n3. Ask whether there is a pay-to-play requirement for the Series A fund.\n4. Consider whether to invest your pro-rata share in the Series B if you believe in the company, since new money at ₹250 buys more shares.\n5. For future deals, ask for the same instrument and protection as the lead investor, and push back on full ratchet clauses before you invest.",
